{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And you shall write upon them all the words of this Torah</b>. Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 27:1) in the name of the Gaon that they wrote upon them the enumeration of the commandments, like the inscriptions in Halakhot Gedolot, in the manner of admonitions. And the meaning of <b>ba'er hetev</b> (Deuteronomy 27:8) is \"write it clearly.\" And our Rabbis said (Sotah 32a) in seventy languages. And we find in Sefer Tagin that the entire Torah was written upon them, from Genesis until לעיני כל ישראל (\"before the eyes of all Israel\"), in its tagin and its adornments, and from there the tagin throughout the entire Torah were copied. And it is possible that the stones were very large, or that it was by way of miracle." "<b>That you may enter the land</b>. Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 27:3) that HaShem will help you if you have begun to observe His commandments, for this is the first commandment upon their entry into the land. And in my opinion, <b>that you may enter</b> is an allusion to all the words of the Torah: he says that you shall write upon the stones all the words of this Torah as soon as you cross the Jordan, in order that you may enter the land, for it is for the sake of the Torah that you come there. And similarly, <b>that your servant and your maidservant may rest like you</b> and <b>you shall remember that you were a servant</b> (above 5:14–15) — \"your servant and your maidservant may rest like you\" is in order that you remember that you were a servant. Or its meaning is: you shall write upon them all the words of this Torah to serve as a remembrance for you, so that you may enter the land and conquer it and dispossess all those peoples, when you remember the Torah and observe all its commandments."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The references to Sotah 32a and Sefer Tagin are rendered as they appear in the source; the latter is a known rabbinic work dealing with scribal crown-markings (tagin) in the Torah scroll."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:56:10.993347118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}